<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The offline remote's bundle manifest. `tools/dist/cmd.sh` substitutes @VERSION@ and copies this
  in; everything else is fixed.

  A third plist rather than a parameterised one, on the same reasoning the builder's gives: these are
  different *kinds* of thing. The builder declares a document type and the machine does not; this one
  declares neither a document type nor any file access, and is the only one of the three that talks to
  the local network. Three short manifests that each say what is true beat one with three conditions
  in it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>KM Remote</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>KM Remote</string>
    <!-- Distinct from `com.karaokemachine.app` and `com.karaokemachine.packagebuilder`. Two bundles
         cannot share an identifier: LaunchServices keys its whole database on it, and a collision
         means one of them silently replaces the other. -->
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.karaokemachine.remote</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>km-remote</string>
    <!-- Without extension. macOS looks for `<this>.icns` in Contents/Resources.

         The remote's own mark: the theme's second accent, green, the same drawing the machine wears
         in amber and the builder in blue. Written by crates/playback/km-display/examples/icon.rs and staged by
         tools/dist/cmd.sh, which is also what makes this bundle at all. This string is the file's
         name without its extension, so renaming icon/km-remote.icns means changing it here --
         and here only, unlike the builder's, which names its icon three times because it also
         dresses a document type. -->
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>km-remote</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>11.0</string>
    <!-- Music, matching the machine it drives. The remote is the same product's other half, and
         filing it apart from what it controls would read as two unrelated programs wherever the
         system lists both. -->
    <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
    <string>public.app-category.music</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      **The one permission this program genuinely needs, and the only one of the three that needs it.**

      The remote finds a machine by mDNS (`_karaokemachine._tcp.local.`) and then talks to it over
      HTTP on the house network. Since macOS 14 that is a permission with a prompt attached, asked the
      first time a bundle sends to a local address -- and, like the builder's file prompts, a bundle
      has an identity of its own here where the same executable run from a terminal inherits the
      terminal's grant and is never asked.

      Without this string the prompt is a bare request from a program somebody has just unzipped. With
      it, it says what the network is for. It does not remove the prompt and is not meant to.

      **Declining it is not fatal**, which is worth knowing before treating this as a prerequisite:
      browsing, searching, the favorites and the top list are all answered out of this device's own
      copy of the catalog, which is the whole reason the offline remote exists. What stops working
      is discovery, queueing and the catalog refresh.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Remote looks for your karaoke machine on this network, and sends it the songs you queue.</string>

    <!--
      **The camera, for the share page's scanner**, which reads a favorites folder's code off another
      device's screen. Worded identically to the iOS bundle's key in ports/remote/ios/project.yml:
      one product asking one permission should not word it two ways.

      Needed here as well as there because this bundle's window is a WKWebView too, and the same page
      runs in it -- `wry`'s permission handler grants the camera, and without this key macOS refuses
      the grant rather than asking. Declining it is not fatal: that page also takes a pasted code,
      and it hides its own Scan button when there is no camera to use.
    -->
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KaraokeMachine Remote reads a favorites folder's code shown on another phone's screen, to copy those songs onto this one.</string>

    <!--
      **No NS*FolderUsageDescription keys here, and that is a difference from the builder rather than
      an omission.** That tool is pointed at a folder of somebody's songs, so it is asked about the
      Desktop, Documents, Downloads and external drives -- it walks a corpus, and a corpus lives
      wherever its owner put it.

      This one opens exactly two files a person chose, and neither needs a grant. Saving a backup is
      a download, so the *browser engine* writes it wherever downloads go and the app never names a
      path; restoring one reads a file the person handed to a file input, which arrives already
      granted. Declaring those four keys would make macOS prompt for access this program never asks
      for.

      No microphone either -- microphone audio is mixed in hardware and nothing here opens an input
      stream, which is why the camera grant above is for `.camera` alone. See the decision in
      docs/decisions/.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
